      "description": "Chalmers uses one assertion relation. Lean allows a separate base theory T₀ whose theorems carry over to T: a proof of σ in T must yield a proof of B(σ) in T₀, and the other belief laws and diagonal equivalences must also be provable in T₀. Taking T₀ = T recovers the one-system setup."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Instead of requiring the resources of Peano arithmetic, Lean assumes diagonalization for every formula directly, though the passage uses only the fixed point for ¬B. This admits languages beyond arithmetic, subject to technical requirements on language size, decidable symbol equality, and self-reference."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Lean models unassailable assertion by classical derivability and belief by a formula satisfying the stated derivability conditions. It does not independently require that this formula describe an agent’s actual beliefs or that the agent possess epistemic knowledge of its soundness."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The target faithfully states the advertised reflection-schema variant: under the belief and diagonalization assumptions, proving every instance of “if I believe σ, then σ” makes T inconsistent. It does not purport to derive inconsistency from (4) alone under those assumptions."
